  "summary": "Traditional Hawaiian garland or wreath made from flowers, leaves, shells, or other natural materials.",
  "description": "Circular arrangement of various materials worn around the neck or head, featuring vibrant colors and fragrant blossoms. Originated as a symbol of aloha and respect, evolving through Polynesian traditions to become Hawaii's most recognized cultural symbol representing love, honor, and celebration.",
  "culturalContext": "The Hawaiian lei — Polynesia's garland tradition at its most coded — strings plumeria, orchid, pikake, maile leaf and shell into Aloha's grammar of honor: graduating lei, birthday lei, funeral lei of ti leaf, May Day (Lei Day, since 1928) court competitions, and hula adornment. The lei greeting ritual (airport flower-sellers) broadcast Hawai'i's image; Aloha Airlines' lei drop-offs and lei-making as school curriculum keep it living craft, and Hawaiian feather lei (hulu) descend from ali'i regalia.",
